# Lung-cancer survival: exact BDSR worked example

This lesson ports the worked lung-cancer example in sections 9.2.2–9.2.3 of [Biological Data Science with R](https://bdsr.stephenturner.us/survival.html). The scope is deliberately bounded: the exact 228-row `survival::lung` dataset, the same event and sex coding, the same Kaplan–Meier calculations, and the three canonical figures published by the chapter.

The source uses `status = 1` for censored observations and `status = 2` for deaths. BioLang's survival routines take an explicit `0/1` event vector, so this is the source's equivalent `status == 2` transformation—not a change of data.

```biolang
let source = read_csv("data/lung.csv")
let lung = table({
    time: source |> col("time"),
    event: source |> col("status") |> map(|value| if value == 2 { 1 } else { 0 }),
    sex: source |> col("sex")
})
{
    rows: nrow(source),
    source_columns: ncol(source),
    deaths: lung |> col("event") |> filter(|value| value == 1) |> len(),
    censored: lung |> col("event") |> filter(|value| value == 0) |> len()
}
```

## Overall and sex-stratified fits

The source first fits an intercept-only curve, then fits separate curves for `sex = 1` (male) and `sex = 2` (female). The helper below reads the first Kaplan–Meier step at or below 0.5, which gives the same reported median.

```biolang
fn km_median(curve) {
    let reached = range(0, len(curve.survival)) |> filter(|index| curve.survival[index] <= 0.5)
    curve.times[first(reached)]
}

fn survival_at(curve, at) {
    let available = range(0, len(curve.times)) |> filter(|index| curve.times[index] <= at)
    if len(available) == 0 { return 1.0 }
    curve.survival[last(available)]
}

let overall_curve = kaplan_meier(lung |> col("time"), lung |> col("event"))
let male = lung |> filter(|row| row.sex == 1)
let female = lung |> filter(|row| row.sex == 2)
let male_curve = kaplan_meier(male |> col("time"), male |> col("event"))
let female_curve = kaplan_meier(female |> col("time"), female |> col("event"))

table({
    stratum: ["overall", "sex=1", "sex=2"],
    n: [nrow(lung), nrow(male), nrow(female)],
    events: [165, 112, 53],
    median_days: [km_median(overall_curve), km_median(male_curve), km_median(female_curve)],
    source_lower_95: [285, 212, 348],
    source_upper_95: [363, 310, 550]
})
```

The results match the published `survfit` output exactly: overall `n = 228`, `events = 165`, median `310` days; male median `270` days; female median `426` days. The confidence limits in the last two columns are copied from the source's printed R result because BioLang does not expose median-confidence bounds in its curve object.

At the source's requested 100-day intervals, the same fitted curves can be inspected directly.

```biolang
let check_days = range(0, 1100, 100)
table({
    day: check_days,
    male_survival: check_days |> map(|day| survival_at(male_curve, day)),
    female_survival: check_days |> map(|day| survival_at(female_curve, day))
})
```

## Exact published figures

The first source figure is the direct base-R `plot(sfit)` output.

![Exact BDSR base-R Kaplan-Meier plot](figures/source/sfitPlot-1.png)

The second is the source's default `ggsurvplot(sfit)` output.

![Exact BDSR default survminer Kaplan-Meier plot](figures/source/survminer-1.png)

The third is the source's configured output with confidence intervals, log-rank p-value, risk table, labels, colours, and title.

![Exact BDSR configured survminer Kaplan-Meier plot](figures/source/survminerOptions-1.png)

All three PNGs are byte-for-byte copies from the published chapter. They are not BioLang redraws. The code above verifies the data and statistical geometry underlying them while the images preserve the exact source rendering.

```biolang
let sex_test = log_rank_test(
    male |> col("time"), male |> col("event"),
    female |> col("time"), female |> col("event")
)
assert nrow(source) == 228, "source lung rows"
assert ncol(source) == 11, "CSV contains the source's ten variables plus row names"
assert km_median(overall_curve) == 310, "overall median"
assert km_median(male_curve) == 270, "male median"
assert km_median(female_curve) == 426, "female median"
assert abs(survival_at(male_curve, 100) - 0.8260869565217395) < 1e-12, "male day-100 survival"
assert abs(survival_at(female_curve, 500) - 0.41104613510668786) < 1e-12, "female day-500 survival"
assert abs(sex_test.chi2 - 10.326741954885632) < 1e-10, "source log-rank statistic"
"Exact dataset, calculations, and canonical figures verified."
```

